<em>Tom's Guide</em> ran the new iPad mini through its standard battery test, which involves continuous web browsing over Wi-Fi with the display set to 150 nits of brightness, and the device <a href="https://www.tomsguide.com/computing/ipads/apple-ipad-mini-8-review">achieved much better battery life</a> than Apple advertises.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/iPad-mini-OLED-feature-1.jpg" alt="" width="2324" height="1307" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1073139" /><br/>
According to the review, the iPad mini 8 lasted for 14 hours and 24 minutes in this test, up from 10 hours and 36 minutes for the iPad mini 7.<br/>
<br/>
<em>Tom's Guide</em> said it typically performs the battery test three times to get an average time, but it only had time for a single run for this review. However, their review said they suspect the final results will be similar to the first run.<br/>
<br/>
This is an interesting finding, as Apple does not advertise longer battery life for the new iPad mini, with its <a href="https://www.apple.com/ipad/compare/?modelList=ipad-mini-a20pro,ipad-mini-a17pro,ipad-pro-11-m5">tech specs</a> showing "up to 10 hours of surfing the web on Wi-Fi" for both the iPad mini 7 and iPad mini 8. The new model does have Apple's more power efficient C2 modem for cellular connectivity, but <em>Tom's Guide</em>'s test used Wi-Fi.<br/>
<br/>
We will have to see if further tests support this finding, but longer battery life may be an unadvertised benefit of the new iPad mini. The new model <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/31/ipad-mini-8-battery-capacity-mah-wh/">actually has a slightly smaller battery</a> compared to the previous model, but power efficiency gains could come from the A20 Pro chip and the OLED display when showing darker content.<br/>
